\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Assume CH. If an abstract elementary class K in a countable finitary relational language has $LS(K)=\aleph _{0}$ and is categorical in a cardinal above $H(K)=beth_{\omega _{2}}$, must it be categorical in H(K), without amalgamation, tameness or joint-embedding assumptions?

\subsection{Short English statement}
Assume CH. If an abstract elementary class K in a countable finitary relational language has $LS(K)=\aleph _{0}$ and is categorical in a cardinal above $H(K)=beth_{\omega _{2}}$, must it be categorical in H(K), without amalgamation, tameness or joint-embedding assumptions?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] Original question source or mathematical context.
\url{https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=List_of_unsolved_problems_in_mathematics&oldid=1366636767#Model_theory_and_formal_languages}.
