\section*{Downward categoricity transfer to the Hanf threshold under CH}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 240; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that under CH, an abstract elementary class of countable L\"owenheim--Skolem number can be categorical on a tail while failing categoricity at $\beth_{\omega_2}$, refuting the prescribed lower-threshold transfer.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 240; 2026-09-24), countable finite-subset diagrams carry color and order data through coherent strong inclusions. Partition bounds forbid an uncountable base pattern and force uniqueness on the tail, while two explicit structures retain different patterns at the proposed endpoint.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The displayed question has a negative answer under the manuscript's stated construction. Its counterexample meets the conditions in this problem and defeats the universal assertion, so this is a full disproof claim. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 240; 2026-09-24).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 240; 2026-09-24), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: under CH, an abstract elementary class of countable L\"owenheim--Skolem number can be categorical on a tail while failing categoricity at $\beth_{\omega_2}$, refuting the prescribed lower-threshold transfer.

The central assertion is within the documented formal scope; the additional applications retain their own scope limits.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{CHObstruction.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.CHObstruction.main}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

